 Cleaning out the notebook as prep work continues for Axios BFD , next Tuesday in New York City. 

 😷 Contagion concerns: Anthropic's IPO is just a week away, and is suddenly trying to dodge Nvidia fallout .

 The macro is a possible unwind, or at least reorientation, of the AI trade. The micro is a myriad of financial and strategic ties between the two companies. Nvidia agreed to purchase around $10 billion of Anthropic shares via a $26 billion private placement concurrent to the IPO. That ties it with Google for the largest investor in that deal, and is in addition to a $10 billion funding commitment that Nvidia made to Anthropic late last year . Anthropic also has committed hundreds of billions of dollars to cloud computing deals for Nvidia GPUs, including with SpaceX, Microsoft, Nscale, and Lambda. On the upside, Jensen Huang was a very vocal cheerleader for open-source AI, so his ouster could give some boost to closed model makers like Anthropic. There is no indication that Nvidia wouldn't make good on any of its pledges, and the Anthropic IPO is said to be oversubscribed (although we don't know at what price), but we're obviously in uncharted territory. And there's a new WSJ report suggesting that interim CEO Colette Kress was worried that Jensen ventured out over his skis on certain dealmaking.

 At the very least, Anthropic IPO investors should view the prospect of future mega-deals with Nvidia as hazy. 📺 Coming attractions: Maybe some of this will come up at noon ET today, when Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei has a livestreamed conversation with JPMorgan CEO Jamie Dimon.

 📰 The latest: Nvidia shares opened up about 2% this morning, which would put it close to early Tuesday trading levels — two days before Jensen resigned — and down around 11% from last Friday's open. 

 Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent on CNBC this morning threw Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick under the bus, saying that Treasury, State, Defense, and USTR all wanted to put Megaspeed on the restricted entities list "for months, but Commerce has the pen." Not surprising, given the confrontation Axios recently reported between Bessent and Lutnick. There are bipartisan calls for Jensen and Lutnick to testify in front of the Senate during its lame duck session, with Lutnick having already agreed. New questions also are being raised as to how certain the U.S. government is that Alice Huang was a spy, and who might have leaked the classified State Department memo to that effect. Again, this could become as much a political scandal as a business one. 🇯🇵 Eastern horizon: Last year we wondered if Japan's election of Sanae Takaichi as prime minister would cool the country's dealmaking boom.

 The early verdict is that it has, but only slightly. Japan M&amp;A activity is around $140 billion year-to-date, down 32% from this point in 2025. But that's still blowing away the prior 15 years, only one of which (2020) even breached the $100 billion mark YTD. Most of the decline has come domestically and strategically, as cross-border M&amp;A is only down 9% and PE-backed M&amp;A is down 15%. 
 
