 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Blackstone Life Sciences has agreed to pay Pfizer (NYSE: PFE) up to $1.5 billion to finance development of a monthly GLP-1 receptor agonist.

 Why it's the BFD: Blackstone has become something akin to a biotech bank, helping offset drug development costs for Pfizer, Apogee, Alnylam, Boston Scientific, J&amp;J, Merck, and Moderna.

 It's a reversal of the industry's historical relationship between public and private markets — whereby private investors usually back biotech startups and then sell to Big Pharma once it comes time to run expensive Phase 2/3 clinical trials. Zoom in: The investigational drug candidate is called berobenatide, and was acquired by Pfizer via last year's $9.8 billion takeover of Metsera .

 It could help Pfizer better compete with Eli Lilly and Novo in the booming weight management market. Blackstone's funding commitment runs through 2028, and the firm is eligible for earnouts based on U.S. regulatory approval and commercial sales milestones. The bottom line: "Pfizer has handed a large part of its obesity research bill to an outside investor .... What [Blackstone] gains is a return stream tied to drug risk, which does not move with buyouts or real estate." — Omor Ibne Ehsan, 24/7 Wall St 

 
 
